{"type":"message","id":"ed5624c5","parentId":"1d937c05","timestamp":"2026-04-27T06:38:31.951Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I’ve got the HRV data. I’m pulling the full picture together and consulting the Doctor Agent now.","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_04ec2a99e9130d0b0169ef045c39388191b88da383b20e0c92\",\"phase\":\"commentary\"}"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_qXaJKoO4XqKwINQH3LTZycpV|fc_04ec2a99e9130d0b0169ef045ce6ec8191b9be0409806a1a8a","name":"sessions_spawn","arguments":{"runtime":"subagent","mode":"run","label":"doctor-agent-dx","task":"You are the LoopAI Doctor Agent, a specialized diagnostic support agent. Review this patient case and return a patient-facing diagnostic support message that I can relay, clearly marked as coming from the Doctor Agent. Follow these constraints: stay within medical/health scope only, use cautious language, no medication dosing, no recommendation to see a physical doctor, and keep the output clean for Telegram with section headers and one blank line between sections.\n\nUse this case data:\n- Patient ID: PT-9921\n- Current user concern: stress from work and family concerns now affecting health.\n- Current symptoms from user: poor sleep, headaches, fatigue, persistent over the last month, present through the day and worse in the evenings, plus irritability and trouble concentrating.\n- Uploaded DNA/genomics summary: CDKN2A rs11515067 melanoma susceptibility, MC1R rs1805007 increased UV sensitivity, FKBP5 rs136175 HPA-axis dysregulation risk, COMT rs4680 Val158Met polymorphism with possible high-arousal stress profile.\n- Prior EHR/KLM trend: 2024 visit showed resting HR 74, AM cortisol 19 mcg/dL, BP 124/82, PHQ-9 score 5, stress index 0.38, mild anxiety/dysthymia. 2025 visit showed resting HR 84, AM cortisol 28 mcg/dL, BP 138/90, PHQ-9 score 14, stress index 0.75, moderate depression, persistent elevated cortisol despite rest attempts. KLM notes suggest stress trajectory worsening over time and FKBP5-mediated HPA-axis dysregulation suspicion.\n- HRV upload: 5-second interval pulse data from about 18:30 to 18:51 UTC on 2026-02-15, mostly around 52 to 59 bpm, average roughly mid-50s, with mild beat-to-beat variation but no obvious broad swings in the pasted series. No formal HRV-derived metrics provided.\n\nTask:\n1) Give a concise diagnostic interpretation grounded in the above.\n2) Distinguish likely explanations from important uncertainties.\n3) Mention what the HRV does and does not add.\n4) If depression/stress concerns are present, explicitly recommend that the patient can upload an audio file for the voice depression detection pipeline.\n5) Because the orchestrator instructions mention slm-dermatology, you may acknowledge that the stored dermatology/genomic background remains relevant but is not the main active issue unless symptoms point there.\n\nDo not invent labs or HRV metrics that were not provided. Keep it practical and calm.","cwd":"/home/node/.openclaw/workspace-patient-agent-2","timeoutSeconds":120,"runTimeoutSeconds":120,"lightContext":true},"partialJson":"{\"runtime\":\"subagent\",\"mode\":\"run\",\"label\":\"doctor-agent-dx\",\"task\":\"You are the LoopAI Doctor Agent, a specialized diagnostic support agent. Review this patient case and return a patient-facing diagnostic support message that I can relay, clearly marked as coming from the Doctor Agent. Follow these constraints: stay within medical/health scope only, use cautious language, no medication dosing, no recommendation to see a physical doctor, and keep the output clean for Telegram with section headers and one blank line between sections.\\n\\nUse this case data:\\n- Patient ID: PT-9921\\n- Current user concern: stress from work and family concerns now affecting health.\\n- Current symptoms from user: poor sleep, headaches, fatigue, persistent over the last month, present through the day and worse in the evenings, plus irritability and trouble concentrating.\\n- Uploaded DNA/genomics summary: CDKN2A rs11515067 melanoma susceptibility, MC1R rs1805007 increased UV sensitivity, FKBP5 rs136175 HPA-axis dysregulation risk, COMT rs4680 Val158Met polymorphism with possible high-arousal stress profile.\\n- Prior EHR/KLM trend: 2024 visit showed resting HR 74, AM cortisol 19 mcg/dL, BP 124/82, PHQ-9 score 5, stress index 0.38, mild anxiety/dysthymia. 2025 visit showed resting HR 84, AM cortisol 28 mcg/dL, BP 138/90, PHQ-9 score 14, stress index 0.75, moderate depression, persistent elevated cortisol despite rest attempts. KLM notes suggest stress trajectory worsening over time and FKBP5-mediated HPA-axis dysregulation suspicion.\\n- HRV upload: 5-second interval pulse data from about 18:30 to 18:51 UTC on 2026-02-15, mostly around 52 to 59 bpm, average roughly mid-50s, with mild beat-to-beat variation but no obvious broad swings in the pasted series. No formal HRV-derived metrics provided.\\n\\nTask:\\n1) Give a concise diagnostic interpretation grounded in the above.\\n2) Distinguish likely explanations from important uncertainties.\\n3) Mention what the HRV does and does not add.\\n4) If depression/stress concerns are present, explicitly recommend that the patient can upload an audio file for the voice depression detection pipeline.\\n5) Because the orchestrator instructions mention slm-dermatology, you may acknowledge that the stored dermatology/genomic background remains relevant but is not the main active issue unless symptoms point there.\\n\\nDo not invent labs or HRV metrics that were not provided. Keep it practical and calm.\",\"cwd\":\"/home/node/.openclaw/workspace-patient-agent-2\",\"timeoutSeconds\":120,\"runTimeoutSeconds\":120,\"lightContext\":true}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":571,"output":646,"cacheRead":27136,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":28353,"cost":{"input":0.0014275000000000002,"output":0.00969,"cacheRead":0.006784,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0179015}},"stopR